{"name":"Snowshoe Hare","hex":"#e7e3d6","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"snowshoe hare","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A North American hare (Lepus americanus) that has large, furry feet; its fur is white in winter and brown in summer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/snowshoe_hare"}],"entries":[{"title":"Snowshoe hare","blocks":[{"text":"The snowshoe hare, also called the varying hare or snowshoe rabbit, is a species of hare found in North America. The \"snowshoe\" moniker refers to the hare's large hind feet, which prevent it from sinking into soft snow, much like snowshoes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Lepus_americanus_5459_cropped.jpg/330px-Lepus_americanus_5459_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snowshoe_hare","wikidata":"Q81063"},{"title":"Snowshoe","blocks":[{"text":"Snowshoes are specialized outdoor gear for walking over snow. Their large footprint spreads the user's weight out and allows them to travel largely on top of rather than through snow. Adjustable bindings attach them to appropriate winter footwear."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Atlas_snowshoes.jpg/330px-Atlas_snowshoes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snowshoe","wikidata":"Q214724"},{"title":"Hare","blocks":[{"text":"Hares are mammals belonging to the genus Lepus. They are herbivores and live solitarily or in pairs. They nest in depressions in the ground called forms, and their young are able to fend for themselves shortly after birth. The genus includes the largest lagomorphs. Most are fast runners with long, powerful hind legs, and large ears that dissipate body heat. Hare species are native to Africa, Eurasia and North America. A hare less than one year old is called a leveret. A group of hares may be called either a husk or a down."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Scrub_Hare_%28Lepus_saxatilis%29_close-up_%2830544290256%29_%282%29.jpg/330px-Scrub_Hare_%28Lepus_saxatilis%29_close-up_%2830544290256%29_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hare","wikidata":"Q46076"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Snowshoe_Hare%2C_Shirleys_Bay.jpg/500px-Snowshoe_Hare%2C_Shirleys_Bay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Snowshoe_Hare%2C_Shirleys_Bay.jpg/1280px-Snowshoe_Hare%2C_Shirleys_Bay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Snowshoe_Hare%2C_Shirleys_Bay.jpg/500px-Snowshoe_Hare%2C_Shirleys_Bay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASnowshoe_Hare%2C_Shirleys_Bay.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Die_S%C3%A4ugthiere_in_Abbildungen_nach_der_Natur%2C_mit_Beschreibungen_%28Plate_CCXXXIV.B%29_%288610042820%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Die_S%C3%A4ugthiere_in_Abbildungen_nach_der_Natur%2C_mit_Beschreibungen_%28Plate_CCXXXIV.B%29_%288610042820%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Die_S%C3%A4ugthiere_in_Abbildungen_nach_der_Natur%2C_mit_Beschreibungen_%28Plate_CCXXXIV.B%29_%288610042820%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Die_S%C3%A4ugthiere_in_Abbildungen_nach_der_Natur%2C_mit_Beschreibungen_%28Plate_CCXXXIV.B%29_%288610042820%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Engraving of the snowshoe hare by Johann Nufsbiegel","link":"https://en.wik